    "title": "Enum methods via impl blocks on algebraic types.",
    "code": "// Enum methods via impl blocks on algebraic types.\n//\n// Forge lets you attach instance methods to algebraic `type`\n// definitions. The first parameter is named `it`, and method\n// dispatch walks through the ADT's `__type__` field into the\n// registered method table. Match expressions inside method bodies\n// destructure variants the usual way.\n\ntype Shape = Circle(float) | Square(float)\n\nimpl Shape {\n    fn area(it) {\n        match it {\n            Circle(r) => return 3.14 * r * r\n            Square(s) => return s * s\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn scale(it, factor) {\n        match it {\n            Circle(r) => return Circle(r * factor)\n            Square(s) => return Square(s * factor)\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn is_circle(it) {\n        match it {\n            Circle(_) => return true\n            _ => return false\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nsay \"Circle(5.0).area() = \"\nsay Circle(5.0).area()\n\nsay \"Square(4.0).area() = \"\nsay Square(4.0).area()\n\n// Chained: scale first, then measure.\nsay \"Circle(2.0).scale(3.0).area() = \"\nsay Circle(2.0).scale(3.0).area()\n\n// Predicate dispatch via collection builtin.\nlet shapes = [Circle(1.0), Square(2.0), Circle(3.0)]\nsay \"circles only: \"\nsay shapes.filter(fn(s) { return s.is_circle() }).len()\n"
